  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n\nThe primary concern for this forecast period will be the impacts \nfrom the remnants of Hurricane Isaias this weekend. Hurricane Isaias \nis currently north of the Yucatan Peninsula and is forecast \nstrengthen further over the next 24 hours. The storm will landfall \nsomewhere along the central Gulf Coast sometime Friday evening-night \nbefore turning northwest. The remnant tropical low will move into \nwestern Tennessee by Saturday evening. This event will be another \ngreat example of impacts occurring away from the track of the \nsurface low. Gusty winds and moderate to heavy rainfall will begin \nSaturday morning and continue into Sunday. The good news is that \nthis storm will move through fairly quickly as it will begin to \nphase with an upper level trough moving through NE NOAM. This will \nlimit the potential for heavy rains and flooding. \n\nLet's breakdown the expected impacts from the remnants of Hurricane\nIsaias for the region for Saturday and Sunday:\n\nHeavy Rain Potential: There will be potential of locally heavy \nrainfall beginning Saturday morning for mainly the Plateau, \nsoutheast Tennessee, and southwest North Carolina. Rainfall amounts \nwill range between 1 to 2 inches with locally higher amounts up to \n3\" possible in SE TN. There will be an area of lighter amounts where \ndownslope winds across the northern and central Tennessee valley act \nto limit the potential QPF amounts. A Flood Watch goes into effect \nSaturday morning for portions of SE TN and SW NC. \n\nHigh Winds Potential: The first corridor of high wind potential \nexists across SE TN and in and around the Chattanooga area. Wind \ngusts up to 50 mph will be possible in these areas. Higher \nelevations around the Chattanooga area could see gusts by daybreak \nSaturday in excess of 60 mph, especially Lookout and Signal \nmountains.\n\nThe second corridor of high winds will be along the eastern TN \nmountains and foothills. The RRFS highlights the potential of high \nwinds with peak gusts up to 80 mph in the mountains Saturday. \nMountain wave high winds look likely for the far east Tennessee \nmountains and foothills given the pressure gradient in place between \nIsaias and a wedge of high pressure in the Carolinas. The potential \nfor wind gusts between 60 to 80 mph is possible Saturday into Sunday \nmorning. A High Wind Watch goes into effect for Saturday morning \nthrough Sunday morning to address the potential high winds. \n\nFor the rest of east Tennessee, southwest Virginia, and southwest \nNorth Carolina: Surface winds of 15 to 30 mph with gusts up to 45 \nmph are possible Saturday and Sunday. What about the potential for \nsevere weather? On Saturday, instability is sparse and elevated that \nshould limit any thunderstorm development across our area. On \nSunday, remnants of Isaias moves north and northeast of the region \nwith chance of showers continuing. The dry slot may allow some \nlimited instability to build in and support an isolated thunderstorm \nchance. The potential for severe thunderstorms remains very low. The \nremnants of Isaias will quickly move northeast into the Ohio Valley \nSunday night.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, we will see the return of upper \nlevel ridge as it builds into the region. This will return a\nprolonged stretch of dry, mostly sunny weather for much of next\nweek. High temperatures will run a few degrees warmer than normal\nwith limited precipitation chances through mid-week.",
